.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 29, 2026
.Dt NEWSYSLOG 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm newsyslog
.Nd rotate log files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl CFNnrsv
.Op Fl a Ar directory
.Op Fl d Ar directory
.Op Fl f Ar config_file
.Op Fl S Ar pidfile
.Op Fl t Ar timefmt
.Op Ar
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility keeps log files from growing without bound.
For each log named in
.Xr newsyslog.conf 5
it decides whether the log is due: whether it has reached its size limit, or
its interval or rotation time has come since it was last rotated.
A due log is rotated: its archives are renumbered, the oldest beyond the
configured count removed,
.Ar file
becomes
.Ar file Ns .0 ,
and a new, empty
.Ar file
is created with the configured owner, group and mode.
Unless the entry has the
.Cm B
flag, the new file begins with a line saying the log was turned over.
.Pp
Once every log has been looked at, each daemon whose log was rotated is
signalled once: by default
.Xr syslogd 8 ,
through
.Pa /var/run/syslog.pid ,
with
.Dv SIGHUP ,
so that it reopens its files.
Then the new archives are compressed, if their entries say so, with
.Xr gzip 1
or
.Xr bzip2 1 .
.Pp
When a log was last rotated is the modification time of its newest archive,
which
.Nm
sets to the time of rotation.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl a Ar directory
Put archives in
.Ar directory ,
which must exist; a relative
.Ar directory
is taken from each log's own directory.
.It Fl C
Create each missing log whose entry has the
.Cm C
flag.
Given twice, create every missing log.
.It Fl d Ar directory
Prefix every log file name with
.Ar directory ,
for logs under another root.
.It Fl F
Rotate every log, due or not.
.It Fl f Ar config_file
Read
.Ar config_file
instead of
.Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf .
.It Fl N
Do nothing but what
.Fl C
asks for.
.It Fl n
Print what would be done, and do nothing.
.It Fl r
Run without superuser privileges.
Without this option, or
.Fl n ,
.Nm
refuses to run as another user.
.It Fl S Ar pidfile
Signal the process named in
.Ar pidfile
for entries that name no pid file, instead of
.Xr syslogd 8 .
.It Fl s
Signal no process.
.It Fl t Ar timefmt
Name archives with the time of rotation, in the
.Xr strftime 3
format
.Ar timefmt ,
instead of numbering them.
An empty
.Ar timefmt ,
or
.Ql DEFAULT ,
is
.Ql %Y%m%dT%H%M%S .
.It Fl v
Say what is being done.
.El
.Pp
If
.Ar file
arguments are given, only those logs are looked at.
A file that no entry names takes the settings of the
.Ql <default>
entry, if there is one.
.Pp
.Nm
runs from
.Xr cron 8
every hour, and at boot as
.Ql newsyslog -CN ,
from
.Pa /etc/rc.d/newsyslog ,
to create missing logs before
.Xr syslogd 8
starts.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /var/run/syslog.pid" -compact
.It Pa /etc/newsyslog.conf
the configuration
.It Pa /var/run/syslog.pid
the process ID of
.Xr syslogd 8
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Rotate the system log now, whatever its size:
.Pp
.Dl newsyslog -F /var/log/messages
.Pp
Show what the next hourly run would do:
.Pp
.Dl newsyslog -nv
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bzip2 1 ,
.Xr gzip 1 ,
.Xr newsyslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr syslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in MIT Project Athena, and in
.Fx 2.0 .
OxideBSD's follows
.Fx Ns 's
options and configuration format; it is OxideBSD's own, written in Rust.
.Sh BUGS
The
.Cm X
and
.Cm Y
flags fail, leaving the archive uncompressed, while
.Xr xz 1
and
.Xr zstd 1
are not installed.
The
.Cm D
flag is accepted and does nothing: there are no file flags yet.
